1. JP IT Solutions & Consulting — Kavala, Greece

JP IT Solutions & Consulting

The Game Development Intern listing says the intern would work with developers and designers on a game project, including game mechanics, asset creation and professional game-production workflows. Unreal Engine is mandatory, with C/C++/C# and Unity listed as relevant technologies. It also mentions level design, environment design, gameplay prototyping, Blender/3D modelling and animation.

Products and work

The company appears to work across IT and software rather than only game development. Its public services include:

Software development
IT consulting
Websites
AI services
Data and reporting
Automation
Customer-service and community solutions
Procurement and inventory systems
HR/workforce systems
Finance and contract solutions
Security and identity solutions
ERP and technical solutions
Communication and collaboration tools

One notable product is Handlet™, an ecosystem built around battery-powered Raspberry Pi devices, computer covers, accessories, 3D models and tutorials. Another is ConfidentialPath™, which is designed to connect AI systems, APIs and automations through a controlled and auditable workflow.

Why this is relevant to me

The game internship is particularly relevant because I am studying Software Development and have an interest in game design and horror game development. The position would allow me to develop programming skills while also gaining experience with game mechanics, level design and professional development workflows.

2. PRIOT Digital Systems — Maribor, Slovenia

PRIOT Digital Systems

PRIOT is a software development and digital-systems company that builds web, mobile, AI, IoT and digital-twin solutions. The company works with businesses to create custom software, from early prototypes through to complete platforms.

Products and work

Some examples of PRIOT's projects include:

Nordic Animal Tracking Platform — tracks livestock using IoT devices, live locations, alerts and mapping.
Sparkasse banking platform — helped streamline banking processes and improve the customer experience.
Digital Slovenian Ethnological Lexicon — an online platform for preserving and presenting Slovenian cultural information.
Smart Sample Handling Platform — a robotics-based system for automatically storing, retrieving and tracking laboratory samples.
Concrete Laboratory Platform — automates concrete quality measurements, data collection and reporting.
Connected Animal Platform — uses real-time IoT data for livestock tracking and analytics.
Forest Monitoring — uses AI and satellite imagery to monitor forest health and predict infestations.
DigiSwim — a digital platform for managing and scoring swimming competitions.
HiFlyer — a cloud platform for managing ski-jumping seasons, athletes and live results.

PRIOT also develops its own products, including InventoryManager, OrderManager, OrderVision, TenderEye and Solidlab.

The company's technology work includes Django, ASP.NET, Vue.js, React.js, Nuxt.js, WebSockets and Docker, alongside IoT, sensors, data processing and distributed systems.

Why this is relevant to me

This is probably the best overall match for my Software Development course because the company works with web applications, databases, APIs, UX/UI, AI, IoT and cloud-based systems. My experience with JavaScript, HTML, CSS, SQL, databases, Git/GitHub and full-stack development gives me a foundation that I could build on during the internship.

3. BMI Leisure — Geel, Belgium

BMI Leisure

BMI Leisure specialises in software for Family Entertainment Centres (FECs) and go-karting tracks. Its ErasmusIntern listing states that the company has an international development team of around 30 professionals and has been developing these solutions for more than 20 years.

Products and work

BMI Leisure's product suite includes:

Point of Sale (POS) systems
Office Management tools
Entertainment TV solutions
Online Booking platforms
Customer Portals
Self-Booking Kiosks
Mobile applications
Reporting tools
Timing software

A particularly interesting part of the company is its hardware/software integration.

For example, its systems can communicate with:

RFID gate-access systems — an RFID bracelet can be checked to determine whether a customer has credits and whether access should be granted.
Karting timing decoders — software receives timing information to calculate lap times.
Credit-card terminals — software integrates with payment hardware.
Cash dispensers and other external systems.

Possible intern projects include developing an RFID solution for driver changes in karting endurance races, integrating QR/RFID gate-access systems, developing karting timing-decoder integrations and helping establish QA/testing environments for integrated devices.
